# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is the example and must never
# hold a real key -- master spec rule #13.
#
# THERE ARE TWO KINDS OF VARIABLE HERE AND THEY CARRY DIFFERENT RISK.
#
#   Server-side (VISION_*, OPENROUTER_API_KEY) reach only the relay, a process you run. The
#   key never leaves the machine it is set on. That is the entire reason the relay exists.
#
#   VITE_-prefixed vars are INLINED INTO THE CLIENT BUNDLE and readable by anyone who opens
#   the page. Acceptable for a laptop at a booth. NOT safe for a hosted deployment -- and this
#   repo already publishes to GitHub Pages, so a key added there reaches the internet. Route
#   model calls through a server-side relay before any key goes near a deployed build.
#
# Nothing loads this file automatically. The relay is deliberately dependency-free (no dotenv),
# so it is read by Node itself:
#
#   npm run relay          # hosted, reads .env via --env-file
#   npm run relay:local    # local Qwen, needs no key and ignores everything below
#
# Running `node server/vision-relay.mjs` bare reads your shell environment only, not this file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vision relay (server/vision-relay.mjs) -- Unity only. The web app never calls it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which upstream answers. `openrouter` (default) or `ollama`.
# ollama needs no key, no network, and no account -- it is the offline path.
VISION_UPSTREAM=openrouter

# Required when VISION_UPSTREAM=openrouter. Free account at https://openrouter.ai/keys
# Usage is effectively free: a handful of identifications cost fractions of a cent.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Optional. Extends the model allowlist without a code change, for when an ID drifts.
# Comma-separated. Check what actually exists before guessing:
#   curl -s https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models | grep -o '"id":"qwen/[^"]*"'
# VISION_MODELS=qwen/qwen3-vl-8b-instruct

# Optional. Only if Ollama is not on the default port, or is on another machine.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1/chat/completions

# Optional. The relay binds 0.0.0.0 so the headset can reach it over the LAN.
# PORT=8787

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web app (src/) -- everything below is PUBLIC once built. See the warning at the top.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ElevenLabs — the chef's voice, in two halves that fail independently
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WITH NONE OF THESE SET NOTHING BREAKS. On a laptop the chef speaks through the browser's own
# synthesiser and the only loss is that it sounds like a browser. On the headset it says nothing
# at all and the subtitle carries the answer alone — Quest Browser ships no `speechSynthesis`
# (DECISIONS.md #19), which is exactly the reason the second half below exists.
#
# HALF ONE — THE PRE-GENERATED BARK BANK, in the browser.
# Generated once at load from `core/barks.ts` and played instantly on a cut, because a reaction
# that arrives a second after the knife reads as the game being broken. This key IS inlined into
# the bundle (read the warning at the top of this file), which is tolerable only because it runs
# on a booth laptop we own. Leave it blank on anything deployed.
VITE_ELEVENLABS_KEY=
VITE_ELEVENLABS_VOICE=

# HALF TWO — ON-DEMAND SYNTHESIS, through the relay, for sentences nobody wrote in advance.
# The in-cooking chef answers in text Qwen3 phrased a moment ago, so it is never in the bank
# above, and on the headset there is no browser voice behind it. Without this the whole guidance
# feature is silent on the only device it is built for. See DECISIONS.md #25.
#
#   Browser side (the only variable needed, and it holds no secret):
# VITE_SPEECH_RELAY=/api/speech
#
#   Server side, NOT prefixed VITE_ and therefore never in the bundle. Set these where the
#   server runs. `server/speech.mjs` is mounted at /api/speech by BOTH the Vite dev server and
#   `npm start`, so this is the same path in dev and in production. Both are required: a key
#   with no voice id cannot name an endpoint, and there is no default voice to guess.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
# ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=
#   Optional. Defaults to `eleven_turbo_v2_5`, which is the id the bank above already uses —
#   two ids would mean the pre-written barks and the on-demand answers are two slightly
#   different renderings of one voice, which is audible and reads as a glitch.
# ELEVENLABS_MODEL=eleven_turbo_v2_5
# ELEVENLABS_TIMEOUT_MS=6000
#   Point the relay somewhere else, e.g. a proxy or a local stub. Defaults to the real API.
# ELEVENLABS_BASE_URL=https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1
#   UNVERIFIED if you set it. Left unset the API picks its own format, which is the one the
#   bank has always decoded successfully. A 32kbps mono clip is a quarter of the bytes on venue
#   wifi, but these ids are ElevenLabs' and none has been exercised from this repo.
# ELEVENLABS_OUTPUT_FORMAT=mp3_22050_32

# OpenAI: the counter scan, the "what am I missing" answer, and the end-of-session debrief.
# With no key those three degrade to the local fault text, which is already a complete sentence.
VITE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Optional. Model ids change often; check the current list rather than trusting a default.
# VITE_OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Qwen3 — in-cooking guidance ("hey chef, I don't know what to do next", and the Unsure button)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WITH NONE OF THESE SET THE FEATURE STILL WORKS. Guidance falls back to `localGuidance`, which
# is assembled from `core/steps.ts` and `core/deficit.ts` and is a complete answer, not a
# placeholder — it still speaks, still pins an imperative over the real board, and still writes
# the reasoning on the panel. The model buys phrasing and prioritisation, nothing else. The
# chef's unprompted corrections never use a model at all. See DECISIONS.md #23.
#
# PREFER THE RELAY. Read the warning at the top of this file: a VITE_ key is in the bundle.
# `server/guidance.mjs` holds the key server-side and is mounted at /api/guidance by BOTH the
# Vite dev server and `npm start`, so this is the same path in dev and in production.
#
#   Browser side (the only variable needed for the relay path):
# VITE_GUIDANCE_RELAY=/api/guidance
#
#   Server side, NOT prefixed VITE_ and therefore never in the bundle. Set these where the
#   server runs. No provider is hardcoded — any OpenAI-compatible Qwen3 endpoint works, and
#   only the base URL changes between them:
#     Alibaba DashScope   https://dashscope-intl.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1
#     OpenRouter          https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#     local vLLM / Ollama http://localhost:11434/v1
# QWEN_BASE_URL=
# QWEN_API_KEY=
#   UNVERIFIED default: `qwen3-32b`. Model ids differ per provider for the same weights —
#   DashScope lists bare ids, OpenRouter namespaces them (`qwen/...`), Ollama uses a tag
#   (`qwen3:8b`). Nothing in this repo has called a live Qwen3 endpoint; set this to whatever
#   your provider actually lists rather than debugging a 404 at the table.
# QWEN_MODEL=qwen3-32b
# QWEN_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# Direct from the browser, INSTEAD of the relay. Only for a laptop at a booth with no server
# running: this key is inlined into the bundle and readable by anyone who opens the page. The
# relay above takes precedence if both are set.
# VITE_QWEN_BASE_URL=
# VITE_QWEN_API_KEY=
# VITE_QWEN_MODEL=qwen3-32b
# Per-request ceiling in the browser. A late answer is worse than no answer, so this is short.
# VITE_QWEN_TIMEOUT_MS=6000
